Yes—change the widths to [15:0] and CLA dut to PPA dut. Here is your same testbench for 3(b):

module testbench;

  reg [15:0] A, B;
  reg Cin;
  wire [15:0] S;
  wire Cout;

  PPA dut(
    .A(A),
    .B(B),
    .Cin(Cin),
    .S(S),
    .Cout(Cout)
  );

  initial begin
    $dumpfile("dump.vcd");
    $dumpvars(0, testbench);

    $display("A    B    Cin | Cout S");
    $monitor("%10d %10d  %b  |   %b       %10d",
             A, B, Cin, Cout, S);

    A = 5; B = 3; Cin = 0; #10;
    // Unsigned addition: 8

    A = 4088; B = 8; Cin = 0; #10;
    // Carry propagation across blocks: 4096

    A = 100; B = -25; Cin = 0; #10;
    // Signed addition: 75

    A = 15; B = 15; Cin = 1; #10;
    // Addition with carry-in: 31

    A = -1; B = 1; Cin = 0; #10;
    // Carry-out: Cout = 1, S = 0

    $finish;
  end

endmodule

That adapts your existing 2(b) testbench. To cover all required cases, add these before $finish in both testbenches:

A = 9; B = ~5; Cin = 1; #10;
// Unsigned subtraction: 9 - 5 = 4

A = 2; B = ~(-3); Cin = 1; #10;
// Signed subtraction: 2 - (-3) = 5

Your %d display treats A and B as unsigned, so negative operands will appear as large positive numbers. The stored two’s-complement bits are still correct.

